\f1\fs22 Johnny reached out to Shakti Civil Engineering who quickly produced a machine that could take the disc between jaws on something like a vertical lathe which they attached to an electric motor capable of spinning at over thirty thousand revolutions per minute. Given Johnny was loath to credit anyone but himself with any competence, his was amazed when a fully tested contraption was installed in the bunker before week\'92s end. Determined to cover his awe, he devised a put-down for the engineers, who he\'92d correctly guessed had experience in Shakti\'92s Peacemaking Division (i.e where they designed the truly heinous weapons). \'91You sure this thing won\'92t blow the place up?\'92 he sneered at them, to prevent himself from shamelessly praising their accomplishment.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Come the day of the next test, the technical staff were as nervous as ever as they placed the disc in the machine\'92s cradle, gently tightened the jaws, and adjusted the chuck. Johnny was in a good mood, so decided against pranking anyone.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He wiggled his pear-shaped form into the groaning office chair at the director\'92s station in the control room, fitted his headset, winced as he felt it snag one of his hairs, and submitted to a nervous urge to adjust his coiffure by combing his fingers through the lack of it, and only succeeded in smooshing his skull sweat around a bit. He took a moment to savour all the things and all the plebeians he was in charge of that day, then leaned over his console and flicked a few switches. Lights danced on his instruments as if they worshipped him, and monitors came alive as if he had breathed life into them. \'91Initiate,\'92 he said into his mic, sounding like a graveyard-shift love-song-dedication DJ. \'91Thirty revs per minute.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Thirty revs per minute,\'92 an anonymous technician confirmed\'97Johnny didn\'92t care who.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>All who could view a monitor\'97and those who, in the test bunker, somewhat trepidatiously looked on from behind the protection of many centimetres	of laminated metal-polycarbonate glass\'97held their breaths and watched as a dull leathery disc rotated at about the speed of a whirling dervish. Almost everyone felt comforted by the spinning and, entering a hyperconscious state in which everything but the disc receded from awareness, their hands, head and arms gently rocked as if they were engaging in a reverent ritualistic dance. The techs protected only by the glass, began to revolve in time to the disc, with one foot firm as their other crosses it and propels them round. Almost everyone in the control room pushed back from their consoles and propelled themselves in circles in their chairs, in perfect synch with the disc.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Almost every one seemed to be in an ever-deepening trance. Everyone except Johnny, who was more interested in the multiple aspects of himself dimly reflected in his monitors. He didn\'92t notice how the rotating disc was effecting his team as he instructed, \'91Sixty revs per minute.\'92 When no anonymous tech confirmed his order, he darted angry stares about the control room and saw them all rotating in their chairs, with their hands in the air as they made expressive gestures with their fingers. Johnny transitioned from his graveyard shift DJ voice to his morning shift DJ voice and shouted the order repeated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The techs in the control room came back to their senses, and watched as the disc spun faster while, hidden from any camera angle, the techs behind the glass in the bunker with the disc matched the disc\'92s rotations.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No change in any energy signatures, sir,\'92 a control-room tech reported as dispassionately as she could, even though she was trying to suck up. She knew Johnny loved being called, 
\f2\i sir.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny smiled\'97it was good to get confirmations he\'92d recruited the right brand of sycophant. Then he sneered\'97he despised sycophants. \'91Double the revs,\'92 he commanded, feeling ever more confident.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91One hundred and twenty revs per minute,\'92 a tech reported.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No change in any energy signatures, sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny took a breath of ecstatic satisfaction. \'91Accelerate at sixty revolutions per minute squared,\'92 he said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Aye aye, sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the few minutes it took for the disc to rotate at the speed of a tossed coin, two things happened: the whirling techs behind their laminated metal-polycarbonate protection all tripped over their feet and fell on their arses; and, the ground beneath the facility feet began to tremor ever more violently.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92ll cause an earthquake \'85 sir,\'92 came a nervous cry.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We\'92re getting energy fluctuations.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Whatever the instruments were capturing, Johnny could see with his eyes on the monitor, the disc\'92s surface becoming metallic and refracting ever more intense rainbows on the test chamber walls.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Sir, we have to shut it down.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny gulped. The last thing he wanted was to shut it down, the sense of command and potency he was experiencing. In that moment his insecurities demanded of him an heroic act, which he naturally understood to mean a dangerous act.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The last thing he wanted was to shut this feeling down. So what if the earth trembled before him. And underneath him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For a moment, he dissociated from the present, from the wild and possibly bloodshot eyes of his minions with their pleading mouth and cowering shoulders, and retreated to a comforting memory: his first car\'97which his parents forbade he have because it was a stupid and dangerous toy, but which he bought for himself anyway from his own money, which is to say, from the allowance his parents gave him, which was larger than the annual budgets of many councils and shires of the land. The petrochemical-guzzling multi-litre engine of the haemorrhoid car (so named because at their peak, every arsehole had one) would puff and splutter at low revs, but in his expert teenage hands would become a purring tiger (that is, a threat to pedestrians everywhere) the closer  he jammed the loud pedal to the redline.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When Johnny returned from his reverie, he was certain that machine spinning the disc, or perhaps the disc itself, was merely behaving exactly the same as his first beloved car on a cold morning. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Full steam ahead, Mr Sulu,\'92 he ordered, as if announcing the winner of a dance contest or a squid bake-off.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'92Sorry? \'85 sir?\'92 The earthquake was worsening the faster the disc spun.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s experiencing destabilising resonance at these revs,\'92 Johnny said, mostly because it sounded authoritative and something no one else could refute. \'91It will smooth out the faster it gets. Take us to warp thirty, Mr Sulu.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'92Sir?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You heard me. Thirty thousand revs per minute.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I don\'92t think she\'92ll take that, sir.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Do it anyway.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Now, if any reader should be in doubt that Johnny was\'97while eminently educated and duly decorated\'97a self-entitled, privileged idiot shielded from the cruelties of life since before the time he was an embryo\'97this order should put the matter beyond any question. The difference, after all, between foetal alcohol spectrum disorder by cheap liquor and foetal alcohol spectrum disorder by chardonnay and champagne, is no more than the degree to which rich parents lube the gears of career and destiny for their offspring.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But in a case of the kind of blind luck that only afflicts the gormlessly affluent, the rumblings of ground, office fixture and technical instrument did abate as machine and disc settled into harmonious atunement with their environs and each other. Johnny\'92s minions were amazed sir\'92s brazen irresponsibility was paying off. Johnny chortled; he was accustomed to making blunt, self-assured, self-congratulating intuitive leaps that were self-evidently virtuous, even in the absence of true effort to verify them. They always worked out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Except when they didn\'92t.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The disc never attained thirty thousand revolutions per minute. Well before then, it emitted blue lightening bolts of energy  which bent and shredded everything in the room like tissue paper, including the machine constraining and spinning it. The  concrete test chamber was scorched and charred. The laminated metal-polycarbonate glass observation window held fast, while  the energy etched it with a pattern of galactic fury. Unfortunately, the glass allowed a form of radiation to pass through so intense it was as to a domestic microwave oven as a microwave oven is to match lit at the far end of a polo pitch. The techs behind the glass never stood a chance; their remains resembled less a human form than dirty laundry steeped in\cf0  bouillabaisse boiling in a concrete tub.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With an air of superiority, Johnny said, \'91I think you exceeded the three thousand revs per minute I requested, Mr Sulu.\'92\
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the end, Johnny was relieved they\'92d finally learned something about the disc. They\'92d have to stop getting kinetic on its ass.\

